# Playing a move while reviewing creates a variation and promotes it to the main
# line. This is CLAUDE.md's "SGF Modification and Save" prose scenario made
# executable, and it covers the branch of GameRecord that decides whether the
# recorded result survives — the invariant being that a main line which no
# longer ends in a resignation or a double pass must not keep its RE.
#
# Two shapes, and they take different code paths on purpose:
#   1. an unfinished game is modified in place and the path promoted
#   2. a finished game is *copied* first, so the historical record survives
#
# Coordinates are board columns and rows; board row N is SGF row (size-1-N).
# Both players are human, so nothing responds on its own.

# --- 1. Unfinished game: promoted in place ----------------------------------
# simple.sgf is 19x19, B[dd] W[pp] B[dp] W[pd], no result.
load_sgf tests/data/simple.sgf
wait_idle
switch_player black human
switch_player white human
wait_idle
expect main_line_moves 4
expect has_result false
expect at_end true

navigate_back
navigate_back
wait_idle
expect view_position 2
expect at_end false
expect variations 1          # only the recorded continuation, B[dp]

# A point nobody has played. Two clicks: one to take the stone, one to place it.
click 10 10
wait_idle
expect holds_stone true
click 10 10
wait_until move_count >= 3
wait_idle

# The new move became the main line, so the record is three moves long and the
# original continuation now hangs off move 2 as a sibling.
expect view_position 3
expect main_line_moves 3
expect at_end true
expect phase playing         # promoting a branch is an act of play

navigate_back
wait_idle
expect view_position 2
expect variations 2          # the new move first, the original B[dp] second

# --- 2. Finished game: forked, and the result does not follow ---------------
# resign.sgf is 9x9, B[ee] W[cc] B[gg], RE[W+R].
load_sgf tests/data/resign.sgf
wait_idle
switch_player black human
switch_player white human
wait_idle
expect board_size 9
expect main_line_moves 3
expect phase finished
expect has_result true
expect result black_resigned

# Loading a game that carries a result selects analysis mode, per
# docs/game-modes.md. Leave it: in analysis mode the kibitz engine answers every
# move, which would add a reply on top of the variation below and is covered on
# its own in analysis_mode_auto_reply.scn. Entering MATCH is always permitted —
# only entering ANALYSIS is refused for a human-versus-human game.
expect mode analysis
toggle_analysis_mode
wait_idle
expect mode match

# Rewind one move. The phase leaves Finished; the record keeps its result until
# something actually contradicts it.
navigate_back
wait_idle
expect view_position 2
expect phase paused
expect has_result true

# Play something other than the recorded B[gg]. Because the game carries a
# result, this forks a fresh record rather than editing the finished one.
click 0 0
wait_idle
expect holds_stone true
click 0 0
wait_until move_count >= 3
wait_idle

# The forked game is three moves long and is *not* finished: its main line ends
# in an ordinary stone, so carrying RE[W+R] forward would label a game whose
# recorded moves no longer support it.
expect view_position 3
expect main_line_moves 3
expect has_result false
expect result none
expect phase playing

# There is something to save, and saving clears the flag the Save button reads.
expect unsaved_changes true
expect can_save true
save
wait_idle
expect unsaved_changes false
expect can_save false
